<!--
  The app's notification small icon.

  ## Why this file exists

  Every alarm path in the app used `R.drawable.splash_icon` as its
  `setSmallIcon`. That was wrong, and it is one of the two things that could
  take the process down at the moment an alarm rings.

  `splash_icon` is a `<layer-list>` whose second item is a `<bitmap>` pointing
  at `@mipmap/ic_launcher`. `ic_launcher` is an `anydpi-v26` **adaptive icon**
  (`<adaptive-icon>`), i.e. an `AdaptiveIconDrawable` whose foreground is a
  vector — there is no bitmap to decode. `BitmapDrawable`'s layer inflation
  assumes a bitmap, so resolving that layer is at best an undefined cast and at
  worst a throw *while the notification is being built*. On the alarm path that
  runs inside `AlarmRingService.onCreate()` (a foreground-service lifecycle
  callback) and inside receivers that must not throw — which is exactly the
  "force stop while the alarm is ringing" symptom.

  A small icon must also be a **flat, single-colour, alpha-only** glyph that the
  system renders as a silhouette. An adaptive icon and a full-colour
  `@mipmap/ic_launcher` are both wrong for that slot — which is why
  `CheckInNotifier`, `StreakAlertNotifier`, `WeeklyRecapNotifier` and
  `CompanionNotifier` already use a plain vector (`ic_launcher_foreground`)
  while the four alarm-path notifiers were the odd ones out.

  This is a purpose-built replacement: the standard Material alarm glyph as a
  white vector, which is what the small-icon slot expects. It costs nothing at
  runtime and removes the whole class of failure.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">
